WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.000
With inner change sometimes comes outer change.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:08.440
In The Last Jedi, the second installment of
the most recent Star Wars trilogy, the villainous

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:13.440
Kylo Ren lightsaber-slashes his way to a new
position of power within the First Order:

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.430
Supreme Leader, snatching the title from his
former mentor Snoke after assassinating him

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:19.480
in his throne room.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.699
As a symbolic gesture rejecting Snoke's influence
over him well before that, Ren smashes his

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:29.020
helmet, which is particularly notable given
Snoke's insult:

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.660
"You're just a child… in a mask."

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.840
The Skywalker saga-ending film The Rise of
Skywalker sees Kylo exercising his new authority,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.380
and taking on a modified version of his old
look.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.360
He has the helmet rebuilt from its pieces,
inspired to do so after meeting Emperor Palpatine.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.250
The reworked headgear features jagged silver
bits and red vein-like stripes running across

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:58.750
it, evidence that the smashed parts got welded
back together in a not-totally-precise way.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:03.760
Rise of Skywalker director J.J. Abrams said
the imperfections are an intentional example

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.030
of kintsukuroi, or "golden repair," which
involves repairing broken pottery using visible,

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.030
gold seams.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:14.829
The Japanese technique is particularly appropriate
since Kylo Ren and Darth Vader's costumes

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:17.890
are based on samurai garb. Abrams told Empire:

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:22.650
"Like that classic Japanese process of taking
ceramics and repairing them [...] As fractured

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.329
as Ren is, the mask becomes a visual representation
of that.

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:28.729
There's something about this that tells his
history.

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:32.759
His mask doesn't ultimately hide him and his
behavior is revealed."

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:37.539
Kylo Ren actor Adam Driver likewise revealed
the reason in a recent interview with Entertainment

00:01:37.539 --> 00:01:41.620
Weekly that the helmet is meant to reflect
Kylo Ren's inner conflicts.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:42.620
He said:

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:47.600
"He's a very unformed person, which is exciting
to play and to have that be represented physically

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.159
in a costume piece, or a lighting choice or,
in this case, a helmet.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:54.929
It's a coming together, he's cherry-picked
things he's looked at through his history

00:01:54.929 --> 00:01:57.920
and that he's decided he wants to claim for
who he is.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.310
So it's a physical representation of how that
character has grown."

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:05.009
Kylo Ren has certainly evolved over the course
of the current Star Wars trilogy.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:09.970
He started an ambitious, temperamental young
man who idolized his grandfather, Darth Vader.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.920
He killed his own father, Han Solo, because
he was desperate for power.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.760
But he eventually becomes driven more by a
sense of personal value.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.320
Driver's approach to the character has evolved,
too.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.470
Chatting with CinemaBlend just ahead of The
Rise of Skywalker's release, Driver opened

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:29.190
up about how playing Kylo Ren changed for
him from The Force Awakens to The Rise of

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.190
Skywalker.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.800
He said of working with Abrams, who also directed
The Force Awakens, on the new movie:

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.470
"There was just a shorthand right away that
we didn't have on Force Awakens.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.900
There was no time wasted of, you know, 'Does
he like me?

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:41.820
Am I giving him the right thing?'

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.620
Right away, we started this one with me like,
there was no idea that we can leave off the

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.620
table.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:50.560
Everything that you're thinking and that I'm
thinking, we have to say and try it, because

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.900
we only have this opportunity to do it.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.870
[...] There was already an immediacy and familiarity
with each other that there was no time wasted."

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:02.590
Driver also shared that he experienced a sense
of ownership over Kylo Ren, but never felt

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.120
like he had a full control of him, and never
shut himself off to ideas about where the

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.460
character would go next.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.730
This was helpful going into The Rise of Skywalker
and during the making of the film, which had

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:17.530
huge responsibilities in following up the
artistic vision of Last Jedi director Rian

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.100
Johnson and concluding the decades-spanning
Skywalker saga.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:25.860
With Kylo's arc coming to a close in The Rise
of Skywalker, Driver undoubtedly had to get

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.940
into a certain frame of mind when portraying
the character.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.250
What helped him get there?

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:34.000
There was the cast and crew, whom Driver said
gave him, quote, "ideas in the moment."

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:41.220
And, of course, Kylo's costume certainly informed
his performance, reforged helmet and all.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.460
"Your helmet is so big!"

